   {
    "id": "what",
    "eyebrow": "The structure",
    "heading": "A list of unary numbers, rewritten every round trip",
    "html": "<p>The head runs from end to end; a visit adds 1 to 4 cells at the left end and 3 to 8 at the right. Just after a rightward sweep the tape is a list of blocks of ones separated by single 0s, and almost every block has a length 4k+3: the tape is a list of numbers k<sub>1</sub>, k<sub>2</sub>, ... in unary. The leftward sweep rewrites each block: state D turns each pair <code>11</code> into <code>10</code>, and the left end of a block <code>0111</code> into <code>00010</code>, so a block of 4k+3 ones becomes 2k+1 single 1s, <code>000 1 0 1 0 ... 1 0</code>, and the blocks are separated by <code>000</code>. The rightward sweep zigzags: at every block it backs up a few cells, and over the inside of a block it turns <code>001</code>, <code>010</code>, <code>100</code> into <code>110</code>, <code>101</code>, <code>011</code> (states E, A, B), rebuilding blocks of 4k+3 ones, but not the same ones.</p><p>How the list changes from one round trip to the next has no known formula. Seen at the right end, the first numbers repeat a short cycle (<code>1 0 0 3 1 0 0 ...</code> comes back every few round trips) while the rest looks random: the run is a one-dimensional cellular automaton on the list, seen once per round trip.</p><figure style=\"margin:1rem 0\"><img alt=\"The tape at every visit to the right end, the first 1.5·10&lt;sup&gt;6&lt;/sup&gt; steps, one row per visit, time downwards (dark: 1). The texture is the list of blocks; no large-scale structure appears.\" src=\"data:image/png;base64,\" style=\"width:100%;max-width:900px;height:auto;image-rendering:auto;border:1px solid var(--line)\"/><figcaption style=\"font-size:.9rem;color:var(--fg2)\">The tape at every visit to the right end, the first 1.5·10<sup>6</sup> steps, one row per visit, time downwards (dark: 1). The texture is the list of blocks; no large-scale structure appears.</figcaption></figure>"
   },
   {
    "id": "rules",
    "eyebrow": "Proved in Lean",
    "heading": "The sweep rules",
    "html": "<p>Each row is a theorem of <code>lean/Rules.lean</code>, for every <code>n</code> and every tape <code>L</code>, <code>R</code> (<code>S&gt;</code>: the head in state S on the next cell; <code>(w)^n</code>: n copies of w; <code>0^inf</code>: blank tape).</p><div style=\"overflow-x:auto\"><table class=\"tbl\"><thead><tr><th>rule</th><th>from</th><th>to</th><th>steps</th><th>what it says</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>left_pairs</code></td><td><code>L (11)^n D&gt; 0 R</code></td><td><code>L D&gt; 0 (10)^n R</code></td><td>4 · n</td><td>Leftward sweep: D turns each pair 11 of a block of ones into 10 (4 steps a pair).</td></tr><tr><td><code>left_pairs_E</code></td><td><code>L (11)^n E&gt; 1 R</code></td><td><code>L E&gt; 1 (01)^n R</code></td><td>4 · n</td><td>Leftward sweep in the other phase: E turns each 11 into 01.</td></tr><tr><td><code>right_E</code></td><td><code>L E&gt; (001)^n R</code></td><td><code>L (110)^n E&gt; R</code></td><td>3 · n</td><td>Rightward sweep: E turns 001 into 110 (3 steps a word).</td></tr><tr><td><code>right_A</code></td><td><code>L A&gt; (010)^n R</code></td><td><code>L (101)^n A&gt; R</code></td><td>3 · n</td><td>Rightward sweep: A turns 010 into 101.</td></tr><tr><td><code>right_B</code></td><td><code>L B&gt; (100)^n R</code></td><td><code>L (011)^n B&gt; R</code></td><td>3 · n</td><td>Rightward sweep: B turns 100 into 011.</td></tr><tr><td><code>block_end</code></td><td><code>L 0 1 1 1 D&gt; 0 R</code></td><td><code>L D&gt; 0 0 0 1 0 R</code></td><td>6</td><td>Leftward sweep at the left end of a block of ones 0111: it becomes 00010.</td></tr><tr><td><code>right_end</code></td><td><code>L 0 A&gt; 0^inf</code></td><td><code>L D&gt; 0 0 0 1 0 0^inf</code></td><td>9</td><td>Right end, arrival in A: new cells, and D turns back.</td></tr><tr><td><code>right_end_C</code></td><td><code>L 0 C&gt; 0^inf</code></td><td><code>L D&gt; 0 0 0 0^inf</code></td><td>3</td><td>Right end, arrival in C.</td></tr><tr><td><code>left_end</code></td><td><code>0^inf E&gt; 0 0 1 R</code></td><td><code>0^inf 1 1 0 E&gt; R</code></td><td>3</td><td>Left end: E meets 001, writes 110 and turns right.</td></tr><tr><td><code>a_one</code></td><td><code>L A&gt; 1 0 R</code></td><td><code>L 1 1 B&gt; R</code></td><td>4</td><td>A on a 1 followed by a 0: A1 = 0RD, D0 = 0LE, E0 = 1RA put the 1 back and leave A on the 0 after 3 steps, where the twin 1RB1RA_... gets in 1 step (A1 = 1RA); the fourth step, A0 = 1RB, is the same in both.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"
   },
   {
    "id": "halt",
    "eyebrow": "Halting",
    "heading": "What a halt would need",
    "html": "<p>F1 is the only halting transition, and F is entered only from D on a 1 (writing 0, moving right). So the machine halts exactly when D reads a 1 followed by another 1. In the first 10<sup>9</sup> steps F is entered 112,249,403 times; the cell after was 0 every time. No halt in 10<sup>10</sup> steps.</p><p>Twin of 1RB1RA_1RC0RE_1RD1LB_0LE0RF_1RA0LD_1LD--- (1RB1RA_1RC0RE_1RD1LB_0LE0RF_1RA0LD_1LD---), which differs only in A1 (1RA there, 0RD here). In this run A reads a 1 only when a 0 follows (all 409,996 times in the first 2·10^7 steps), and then 0RD, 0LE, 1RA put the 1 back and leave A on the 0 after 3 steps, where the twin gets in 1 (rule `a_one`): the two machines write the same tapes, this one 2 steps slower each time (the first 2,000 tapes at visits to the right end are identical).</p>"
